			<div class="content" id="post-content-3622879">
									

<p>I wasn't planning on going for a philosophical approach, but now I'm interested. Now, this is going to be quite ad hoc, so bare with me. I'm not sure where you get the idea of Ivan's mind being &quot;copied&quot;, as the dialogue in the first interview states that they &quot;took his consciousness&quot; and placed it inside a computer. From a dualism point of view, the mind can exist without the body, but it's also implied in the dialogue that Ivan's mind wasn't able to exist inside a computer (without his body) so it's kind of &quot;anti-dualism&quot; I guess (correct me if my Philosophy is wrong). The guy in the recording also describes how parts of Ivan's body was added into the submarine to allow him to control it, with Ivan's brain literally placed into the submarine. This is also where his consciousness was centered, effectively lending the submarine almost complete autonomy if it weren't for Peter, and therefor making the submarine Ivan. I guess I didn't explain well enough how Ivan's mind was placed back into the submarine with the rest of his body to allow the submarine to operate.</p>
<p>Imagine it as a medical procedure, with Ivan's mind removed from his body to allow doctors to mix it with the submarine, with the mind being placed back once again and allowing it to operate with a brain (inside the submarine). Forgive me if it's long winded, I'll see if I can clean it up a bit.</p>
<p>Oh and btw, the mention of a &quot;soul&quot; in the final note is simply the man's religious side rearing its head in the note, and isn't meant to be taken as Ivan having a literal soul. And I completely agree about bits of the dialogue being too clunky, like the &quot;etc&quot; which really didn't work after a second reading.</p>
<p>Thanks for the critique anyway.</p>

