														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3622935">
									

<p>Well, my point regarding the transferal vs copying of consciousness is more a reflection of my personal take on the nature of consciousness and the continuity of mind rather than anything that the article explicitly does wrong.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with Ivan's brain literally placed into the submarine</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, if I understand correctly, this occurred after the action described by -2 in the first interview, namely &quot;then they took his consciousness, removed it from his brain and placed it inside a computer&quot;. All I'm saying is that, in my eyes, the act of removing the consciousness from the brain and putting in a computer is itself an act which creates a new consciousness in the process, rather than preserving the old one. I see what you mean by the prolonged process of &quot;moving&quot; Ivan's consciousness around until it was eventually placed in a submarine, but it is the initial &quot;removal&quot; of consciousness from the brain that I consider an act of copying the mind, rather than transferring it, if you see what I mean.</p>
<p>I get the impression from the article that we differ in terms of our conceptions of consciousness, not that I'm a philosopher either. However, I feel that it is implied in the article that consciousness and body are ultimately separable objects, and although you do point out that Ivan's mind is shown to not be capable of living on a computer while remaining lucid, it still seems clear that my idea of consciousness contradicts the article's. Personally, consciousness is the emergent product of all the individual interactions of the brain's neurons, so that without them, the consciousness simply doesn't exist, or isn't the same thing anymore.</p>

							</div>

								
					
