															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608670577" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1510029020 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">07 Nov 2017 04:30</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3627542">
									

<p>This does a lot of things that I like. On the positive side, the interaction between the brothers was emotional and interesting to follow, the mix of biological-ish(shedding, hibernating, growing)/tehcnological/psychic elements of the submarine were fairly interesting, and I like a lot of the little procedural touches you used.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, there were still fairly frequent clinical tone problems and unrealistic dialogue, and, worst of all, you explain <em>everything</em>, in extensive detail, usually more than once. Not only that, these explanations are often serious violations of show-don't-tell. -2 just blurts out the entire motivation and broad method behind the creation of the SCP in a few paragraphs.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-540981-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-540981-1')">1</a></sup> You had someone in an interview log take the time to say, out loud, &quot;Peter, you're sweating. You're hunched over and look like you're about to vomit.&quot; We get the implication that someone got turned into a car, so by god we have to have someone else confirm that.</p>
<p>There is just log after log of people just flat saying everything that's going on. It got to the point where I was so bored of information being presented that I just sort of skimmed the last two logs. Even the actual document between the logs was full of extra detail that didn't advance the story or add interest; much of the extra details in the containment procedures, for example.</p>
<p>I'm not saying everything has to run on mystery and redaction, but almost nothing is interesting enough to support this much documentation or stand up to this much scrutiny.</p>
<p>-1</p>
<p>(P.S. I didn't find this one being cliche as a problem, but towards that claim, we have a <em>lot</em> of GRU-P/Soviet contingency SCPs, including <a href="/scp-1984">SCP-1984</a> and <a href="/scp-3034">SCP-3034</a>.)</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-540981-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-540981-1')">1</a>. The exact method may be lost, but that's nowhere near as important to us as it would be to the Foundation, and we even get tons of that in later notes.</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
